  "handoff": {
    "summary": "Added one high-resolution execution-timing core shared by the REPL, embeddable kernel, and compiler-emitted timed statements. Compilation completes before the measured boundary and result formatting happens afterward. Node reports process CPU user/system/total plus wall time; hosts without process.cpuUsage report wall time only. Cold Python imports and native module loads appear as a nested Initialization tree, while warm repeats omit that section. Compiler-emitted timing now retains its selected finish hook and closes it from `finally`, so exceptions and other abrupt control flow cannot leave an active collector that captures later initialization. Representative output is `CPU times: user 4.741ms, sys: 0.000ms, total: 4.741ms`, `Wall time: 4.010ms`, `Initialization: 3.018ms`, `  import colorsys: 3.018ms`. Focused timing/CST/kernel tests, a clean production build, compiler tests, the 262-test integration tier, strict Python, and architecture checks pass.",
    "risks": [
      "Node CPU measurements use process.cpuUsage and are therefore process-wide; concurrent host work in the same process can contribute CPU time even though the wall boundary is exact.",
      "Initialization attribution currently names Python module imports and first native/runtime requires. Future lazy caches should use the same timing span API when their cost is important to expose.",
      "`pnpm test:changed` selected broad inherited native work and passed build, architecture, unit, strict Python, compiler, integration, and most native checks before an unrelated dense-GF(p) `multiply_300` performance sample exceeded its normalized budget (19.41ms versus 15ms). The timing lane does not change matrix or native code; all exact contract validation commands were rerun with fresh passing receipts."
    ],
    "next_steps": [
      "Integrate this lane, then implement `%timeit` as a separate statistical benchmarking feature built on the shared execution boundary.",
      "Add additional named initialization spans only at measured lazy-loading choke points so ordinary execution output stays concise."
    ]
  }
}
